#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521595} :activity/id #uuid "e8924afe-1a47-5aee-af84-25e72799ddd7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ed3573-aeb0-5cc8-aa92-2d155431b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281776} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1c7147-5d4b-5e81-833d-854efd096d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777} {:answer/id #uuid "194b6ddb-9926-5f0f-9190-d8ff2af941e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281919} {:answer/id #uuid "27c2e034-47f4-59cd-b78d-6e4055cb354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282029} {:answer/id #uuid "b540775c-a7f1-590f-8bcc-78b4486e47c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5a40b87-497b-52fb-93f4-bce3fac9446e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9c2261a-709f-5777-b889-b197f8b76cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-163773" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "4e9223b8-eead-5430-8672-05f220aed1df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a8d6e4-83ad-5779-b6b1-8d3f2f9ddd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474478} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3a7e62-eb4d-5b61-9aa1-0d372465232c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ef37d28-32ff-5d41-97ce-27cbd12aaa3d" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c7161a-6281-5453-ba04-08a2ff9730cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} {:answer/id #uuid "adc00843-8143-55a5-b0fe-475c44f606db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0324d3-98fc-5c1c-a873-f07d8e16c52d" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff8f7eb-17b6-54d7-ade8-e5cacfad8918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474482} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27ba75-5695-5a59-96ba-eacafc25acb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7524397-46db-58f5-a737-2282ef746679" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47992fdc-ac9e-5b34-9ffc-1e1720081dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} {:answer/id #uuid "e264ae91-5911-505d-a745-60ea3f527160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bce15c3-2af0-5965-946b-f94b71b680d6" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e433d738-c2a7-5a16-852b-741bb762e1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-164101" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/02/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)"} :step/id #uuid "5b4f1eb6-06aa-5151-a935-a33c43790429"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434687} {:answer/id #uuid "ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434708} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434729} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163774" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1+\\sqrt{26}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1-\\sqrt{26},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1+\\sqrt{26}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-1-\\sqrt{26}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "5c8783e4-94f5-56ac-a41a-69d2ba5ab312" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48aa8c46-39c6-5468-85c5-ec68e86acbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434676} {:answer/id #uuid "63a1c505-6f99-5e6b-92df-f1c0f1d2c71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434681} {:answer/id #uuid "9984c603-9561-5d15-a7da-151b72f56966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434699} {:answer/id #uuid "d563919d-d0e0-5990-917f-9b961dfd1ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434723} {:answer/id #uuid "ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434728} :answer-field/id #uuid "812379f4-7ca2-54a9-9137-3e6202ac33a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "463ee037-d37e-5536-bfb1-df9c529010e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163776" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1-\\sqrt{5}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =1+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "6003b5aa-8454-5323-bd58-1be79af915b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ace240-ec76-51a3-9237-34363ff229dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433358} {:answer/id #uuid "7c661f49-8f75-57c0-807f-bf1886abf88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433359} {:answer/id #uuid "927103a8-b10f-5218-a094-fe0cd4985992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,0,1)$ and $(0,2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433360} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc9d090-f059-5d47-bb5f-8ea074628146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433367} {:answer/id #uuid "f027110e-c325-53d5-8b9d-70300263dd44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433367} :answer-field/id #uuid "8390ddee-9042-5713-b922-461ba822e40f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e0f0650-f17e-5833-8166-da3fd9367f23" :question/math-academy-id "q-163795" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a39696fcb202485b9a58839609ad807e43397fde8df965bed2824f6cdc307703.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ and  $(x,y)=(1,0),$so the global maximum points are $(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1).$\n\n![](images/4a/4a818d3e3e3c6c6a90ff9e69d808803016114894fbc605de5d21c75817eb8cf3.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d17e39b1-6565-5f9d-b8bf-8cebdb011f05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521599}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520758}]}
